<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>

<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/entry>>

! The relational parameter ~ design law

> A bare arrow standing between two macro parameters names a ''relation'' between them. The parser
> MARKS it; the binder READS it. Nothing that parses today parses differently.

''This rides `dev/sdm-integration` first and reads as a donation second.'' The house builds it as its
own capability; the shape below keeps the upstream door open rather than assuming it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-two-parsers>>

!! TW5 runs TWO parameter parsers, and the live one carries a guard

''THE PATH A `<<name args>>` CALL ACTUALLY TAKES:''

Both `macrocallinline` and `macrocallblock` call
`parseMacroInvocationAsTransclusion`, never `parseMacroInvocation`. The node reads `transclude`, and the
parameters arrive as ''attributes'', positional ones named `"0"`, `"1"`, `"2"` and flagged
`isPositional`.

|! stage |! function |! seat |
|the wikirule | `macrocallinline` · `macrocallblock` | `rules/macrocall*.js` |
|the invocation | `parseMacroInvocationAsTransclusion` | `parseutils.js:216` |
|the parameter list | `parseMacroParametersAsAttributes` | `parseutils.js:302` |
|''one parameter'' | ''`parseMacroParameterAsAttribute`'' | ''`parseutils.js:322`'' |
|the widget | `TranscludeWidget` collects `stringParametersByName` | `widgets/transclude.js` |
|''the binding'' | ''`ParametersWidget` maps declared names onto positions'' | ''`widgets/parameters.js:55`'' |
|the lookup | `getTransclusionParameter(name,index,default)` — name first, then index | `transclude.js:411` |

''THE LEGACY PATH STILL STANDS, and parity wants it moved too.'' `parseMacroParameter`
(`parseutils.js:184`) serves `parseMacroInvocation` (440) and `parseFilterVariable` (478). A change that
touched only one of the two would leave `\function` reading a bare arrow differently from `\procedure`.

⚠ ''THE LIVE PARSER ALREADY GUARDS A COLON, and that precedent decides the sibling fix''
(#/the-uri-scheme). `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute` refuses a named parameter whose name fails
`/^[A-Za-z0-9\-_]+$/`, with the reason stated in the source: //to avoid mis-parsing values like
`$:/foo`//.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/mark-at-parse>>

!! Mark at parse · decide at bind

''THE OBSTACLE THAT DECIDES THE DESIGN.'' TW5 parses a macrocall without knowing what it calls. The
call-site parser holds no view of the definition, so //only procedures that declare an arrow get one//
cannot run at parse time.

''SO THE PARSE STAYS PERMISSIVE AND THE BINDING TURNS SELECTIVE:''

<<~ has Step Parse "does/mark-the-token keeps/position keeps/value adds/relation-field ~ the tree GAINS a field; no tree LOSES one">>
<<~ has Step Declare "does/accept-a-relational-slot where/parseParameterDefinition reads/`\\procedure agent(fires from -> to)`">>
<<~ has Step Bind "does/read-the-mark where/ParametersWidget ~ a procedure that declared a relation reads it; one that did not sees the positional it saw before">>

''THAT SPLIT IS WHAT MAKES THE CHANGE ADDITIVE.'' An undeclared procedure
receives byte-identical parameters before and after. The feature reaches only the definitions that ask
for it — which answers the standing warning that a global operator re-reads other people's files
(#/prior-art).

!!! The chain, and why it needs no new binding model

''DOT SETTLED THIS IN 1991.'' `A -> B -> C` desugars into the adjacent pairs `A->B` and `B->C`; a
subgraph on either side yields the cross-product. A chain reads as ''sugar for adjacent pairs'', never
as a new arity.

So `<<ranks focus a -> b -> c -> d -> e>>` wants no variable-arity binding: it
carries four relations over five terms, and a procedure declaring one relational slot receives the
ordered terms.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-uri-scheme>>

!! The sibling fix ~ a scheme names no parameter

''A `lar:` URI LOSES ITS HEAD IN THE LIVE PARSER.'' `<<agent aim from=lar://a.b.c>>` reads `lar` as a
parameter NAME and `//a.b.c` as its value, because `lar` passes the strict-identifier test and a colon
follows it. Quoting or `[[bracketing]]` the value cures it; both spend syntax on a parser detail.

''THE CURE MATCHES THE GUARD ALREADY THERE.'' The parser refuses `$:/foo` as a name because the name
fails the identifier test. A scheme fails on a different tell: ''a colon followed by `//`''.

''One condition, beside the one that stands.'' Where a name-then-colon reads
with `//` after it, the parser MUST take the whole run as a value rather than a named parameter. That
frees every `lar:`, `http:` and `file:` URI to ride bare — and it stands alone as a donation, wanted or
not by the arrow.

⚠ ''ONLY THE ASCII ARROW STRUCTURES A CALL.'' Measured across the corpus: `→` `↔` `↦` `←` appear
inside calls eighty-one times, and ''every one rides inside a quoted value'' — //relations index poorly
→ factor//, //graceful→force//, //the AI↔operator worldlines//. They read as English, never as
structure. Thirty calls carry BOTH: `->` separating the rungs of a ladder while a glyph arrow describes
a relation in the prose beside it.

''So a rule that read any arrow as relational would tear eighty-one prose values
apart.'' The relational parameter reads `->` `<-` `<->` and nothing else; a glyph arrow carries meaning
for a reader and none for a parser.

⚠ ''THE SIGIL PATH NEVER CARRIED THE FAULT, AND MUST NOT CARRY THE CURE.'' Measured: `<<~ pranala
from=lar:///a -> to=lar:///b >>` splits `from` and `to` with the colons whole, and the generic `<<~ WORD …>>`
hands its entire run through as `p1`. ''Bracketing a URI inside a sigil corrupts it'' — the brackets
survive into the value as `[[lar:///a]]`. Twenty-one corpus sites ride bare there and MUST stay bare.

''AND A NAMED SLOT CURES IT OUTRIGHT.'' Measured: `<<agent aim from=lar:///a.b.c -> to=lar:///d.e.f>>`
and `<<agent yield from=lar:///x -> to=?>>` both render whole, because a named slot puts its `=` ahead
of the scheme's colon, so that colon never stands as the FIRST one the parser meets. The house writes
that form; the arrow keeps its seat between the two slots.
''The fault only ever bit a POSITIONAL URI'' — which is why TW5 core never met it, every URL in its
tree riding a named parameter.

''SO THE BRACKET IS A PARSER WORKAROUND, never a house form.'' It buys a plain-dialect call one render
in a wiki and costs a reader a delimiter that names nothing. ''Both seeds write the chat form bare'' —
a worked turn reads as a transcript, where nothing parses it — and Phase One retires the workaround by
letting the same bare form render.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prior-art>>

!! What the field already decided

<<~ has Art Cypher "stands/FOR gives/visual-correspondence ~ arrows read as relations because the syntax looks like the graph; direction MANDATORY on write, OPTIONAL on read - the `->` `<-` `<->` triple">>
<<~ has Art DOT "stands/FOR gives/chain-desugars-pairwise ~ `A -> B -> C` becomes adjacent edges; no variable arity anywhere in the model">>
<<~ has Art Prolog "stands/AGAINST gives/the-anti-pattern ~ a GLOBAL operator makes other files read differently, and text reads SILENTLY into a different term; the cure the field reached: operators local to a module, imported on purpose">>
<<~ has Art TW5 "stands/PROCEDURE gives/the-house-precedent ~ v5.3.0 met new expressivity with a NEW DELIMITED TOKEN (`<% %>`), never by overloading an existing form">>

⚠ ''THE PROLOG WARNING LANDS ON THE GLOBAL FORM AND MISSES THE DECLARED ONE.'' Marking a token without
re-reading it, and gating the meaning behind a definition, IS the module-local cure transposed.

⚠ ''AND THE TW5 PRECEDENT CUTS BOTH WAYS.'' A new delimiter would read as the house's own habit; an
arrow reads as Cypher's. The arrow wins on legibility and loses on precedent, and the marking design
narrows that gap by leaving every existing form alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/blast-radius>>

!! The radius, measured

|! surface |! count |! reading |
|macro calls carrying a bare `->` in TW5 core · plugins · themes · editions |''0'' | nothing shipped re-reads |
|the same in the house's own plugins |''0'' | nothing local re-reads |
|corpus, plain dialect | 14 | the two seeds alone |
|corpus, sharktooth | 479 | ''a different parser — untouched'' |
|carriers carrying an arrow invocation | 120 | none re-read under a marking design |
|parse-tree → wikitext serialisers in core |''0'' | no round-trip surface |

''THE TW5 FILES THE WORK TOUCHES:''

# `parsers/parseutils.js` — `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute` (322) · `parseMacroParameter` (184) ·
  `parseParameterDefinition` (129)
# `widgets/parameters.js` (55--95) — the binding loop
# `widgets/transclude.js` — `getTransclusionParameter` (411) where a chain resolves
# `parsers/wikiparser/rules/parameters.js` — the `\parameters` pragma shares the definition parser

''THE TESTS THAT MOVE:'' `test-wikitext-parser.js` · `test-html-parser.js` · `test-filters.js`, beside
the branch's own `test-parser-invariants.js`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-boundary>>

!! What the sigil path keeps

⚠ ''A TW5-CORE ARROW DOES NOT REACH `<<~ …>>`.'' The house's sigils parse in `lar-sigil`, emit
''legacy'' `macrocall` nodes, and pass their whole argument run through as one string. They neither
gain the feature nor break under it.

''SO THE SHARKTOOTH ARROW STAYS A SECOND PIECE OF WORK'' — retiring the `pranala` exception
(`lar-sigil-shared.ts`, `lar-sigil.ts`) so any sigil may declare a relation from its grammar tiddler.
It wants the core shape settled first, so the two speak one law rather than two.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-plan>>

!! The plan

<<~ has Phase One "does/free-the-scheme touches/parseMacroParameterAsAttribute risk/low ~ a colon followed by `//` reads as a value; ships alone, wanted by the arrow or not">>
<<~ has Phase Two "does/mark-the-arrow touches/both-parameter-parsers risk/low ~ the token keeps its position and value and gains a relation field; every existing parse stands">>
<<~ has Phase Three "does/declare-the-slot touches/parseParameterDefinition risk/medium ~ `\\procedure agent(fires from -> to)`; the definition grammar grows one form">>
<<~ has Phase Four "does/read-the-mark touches/ParametersWidget+TranscludeWidget risk/medium ~ a declared relation binds; an undeclared one reads as the positional it always was">>
<<~ has Phase Five "does/prove-the-chain touches/tests risk/low ~ pairwise desugaring, direction triple, and a control that an undeclared procedure sees no change">>

''EACH PHASE STANDS ALONE.'' One through four each leave the engine correct and the corpus green;
stopping after any of them costs nothing.

⚠ ''PHASE ONE SHIPS FIRST AND CARRIES ITS OWN ARGUMENT.'' It removes a papercut every URI-bearing
wikitext hits, matches a guard already in the source, and needs no agreement about arrows.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-to-measure>>

!! What the house counts

<<~ has Gauge Additive "counts/parse-trees-that-differ-for-an-undeclared-procedure red/any ~ one difference and the change stops reading as additive">>
<<~ has Gauge Silence "counts/invocations-that-re-read-without-erroring red/any ~ the Prolog failure arrives silent, never as a syntax error">>
<<~ has Gauge Nesting "counts/arrows-marked-inside-brackets-or-quotes red/any-unmarked ~ `Gauge(10 -> 12)` degrades on the gradient; a mark inside a nest reads as a fault">>
<<~ has Gauge Reach "counts/sigil-invocations-that-changed red/any ~ the sigil path stands outside this work by design">>
<<~ has Gauge Standing "counts/arrow-chains-the-seeds-write-unquoted green/all ~ the authored form ALREADY reads as the plan renders it; a quote returning to a chain marks the plan slipping">>
<<~ has Gauge Glyph "counts/glyph-arrows-read-as-structure red/any ~ only the ASCII `->` structures a call; a glyph arrow rides inside a value and MUST stay prose">>

<<~/ahu>>

